{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We have claimed twice now that reading `ages[3]` is instant no matter how big the array is. That claim should feel slightly suspicious — how can finding one slot among ten million take no longer than finding one among ten? This lesson shows the trick, and it is genuinely just arithmetic."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Three facts the machine relies on"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Recall from the memory-model lesson that memory is a long row of numbered slots, and that reading any address costs the same as reading any other. Add two design decisions from our array, and the trick falls out:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "The array knows the address where its first element sits — the **base address**.",
        "Every element is the **same size** in bytes, because they are all the same type.",
        "The elements are **consecutive** in memory, with no gaps."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The calculation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Given those three facts, the address of any element is a single multiplication and a single addition:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "address of element i  =  base address  +  ( i x size of one element )"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Suppose our array begins at address 1000 and holds 4-byte integers. Element 0 sits at 1000. Element 1 sits four bytes later at 1004. Element 3 sits at 1000 + 3 x 4 = 1012."
    },
    {
