    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Notice what the machine did **not** do: it never looked at elements 0, 1, or 2. It never scanned, compared, or searched. It multiplied, added, and read the result. That is three operations whether the index is 3 or 3,000,000 — which is precisely what **O(1)** means."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "What this explains"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Several array rules that look arbitrary are really just consequences of that one formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Why elements must share a type.** The formula multiplies by a single element size. Mixed sizes would make the arithmetic impossible.",
        "**Why the size is fixed up front.** The run of memory has to be reserved as one unbroken block, so the machine must know how much to reserve.",
        "**Why indexing out of bounds is dangerous.** `ages[500]` on a 108-element array happily computes an address and reads it. Nothing about the arithmetic knows your array ended — some languages check and throw, others hand you whatever bytes live there.",
        "**Why inserting in the middle is expensive.** The formula assumes no gaps, so a gap is not allowed to exist. Making room means physically moving elements."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "This is the payoff of learning the memory model first. `arr[i]` is not a lookup — it is a calculation, and every characteristic of arrays follows from that fact."
    }
